name=compare and swap orders the next iteration
%%
// A relaxed read followed by an acquire-release CAS. The loop repeats while
// the CAS fails, so the CAS's write, and the branch on its outcome, are the
// last events of an iteration that has one.
//
// Under sMRD an RMW's write is ordered with its read: what is ordered before
// the release write is ordered before the read, and what the acquire read
// orders after it is ordered after the write too. For the read of *rD in the
// next iteration the acquire read gives the second half, so the CAS's write
// and its branch are ordered before that read, and every event of one
// iteration is ordered before every event of the next.
//
// A CAS's write exists only where it succeeds, where the value read is the
// expected 0. MoRDor's episodicity check used to decide that condition with no
// assumptions at all, under which it never holds, so it gave a CAS none of
// this ordering; a fetch-and-add, whose condition is true, always had it. The
// CAS's write and branch were left unordered against the next read of *rD,
// and the loop was reported not episodic. No other start of the iteration
// helps: one starting at the CAS uses rv before the iteration reads it. The
// check now decides the condition where the write exists.
rC := malloc 1;
rD := malloc 1;
*rC := 0;
*rD := 0;
rs := 0;
do {
  rv := *rD;
  rs := cas(acquire,release,rC,0,rv);
} while (rs = 0)
